// TEST: Field Bounds Crossed
// ============================================================================
// `min=3, max=1` describes a type with NO VALUES. Because `min=`/`max=` are
// inclusive on both ends, there is no reading under which this is a deliberate
// empty range — it can only be a transposition or a sign slip, so it is an
// error rather than a warning.
//
// NOTE THE DELIBERATE ASYMMETRY with the half-open spelling: `in 0 .. 0` is an
// empty range and stays ACCEPTED, exactly as it is at HEAD. That is not an
// oversight. A half-open range whose ends coincide is the ordinary way to
// write "empty" in every `..` context in the language, and empty solution sets
// are a warning-class event in this design, not a failure — Schur-zero
// emptiness is legitimate. `min > max` is different in kind: it is not a way
// of writing empty, it is a way of writing nothing coherent at all.
// ============================================================================
struct Bad2 {
    m: Int<min=3, max=1>
}
// ERROR: BL4003
// ERROR-CONTAINS: bounds cross
